1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,480
Triglycerides — nearly one in three American adults has a level of 150 or higher, and most don't even know it, because high triglycerides rarely cause any symptoms on their own.

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,040
Three everyday habits are usually behind it.

3
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,120
Triglycerides are a type of fat your body stores for extra energy.

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:22,320
Your liver makes them from leftover glucose or alcohol and releases them into your bloodstream.

5
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:30,400
Unlike cholesterol, which responds mostly to saturated fat, triglycerides react much more to sugar, alcohol, and how much you move.

6
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,200
Refined carbs, alcohol, and inactivity are the three biggest drivers.

7
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:40,080
First, refined carbs — think soda, white bread, and white rice.

8
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:48,240
When you take in more glucose than your body needs right away, your liver converts the extra into fatty acids and packages them as triglycerides.

9
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,680
Drinks high in fructose speed up this process even more.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,920
Swap soda for water or unsweetened tea, and trade white rice or bread for whole grains like oats.

11
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:01,840
Cutting back on refined carbs alone can bring your numbers down noticeably.

12
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:03,680
Second is alcohol.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,760
As your liver breaks down alcohol, it ramps up fatty acid production at the same time, and much of that ends up circulating as triglycerides.

14
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,120
Drinking on an empty stomach makes this effect even stronger.

15
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:18,000
Cut back on how often and how much you drink.

16
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:24,080
Skipping the empty-stomach drinking habit first and building in a few alcohol-free days each week both help.

17
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:25,760
Third is inactivity.

18
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:32,800
Your muscles rely on an enzyme called lipoprotein lipase to pull triglycerides out of your blood and burn them for energy.

19
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:38,800
The longer you sit, the less active that enzyme becomes, so triglycerides stay in your bloodstream longer.

20
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,280
Aim for about 30 minutes of brisk walking or similar activity most days.

21
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,160
Moving your muscles reactivates that enzyme and helps clear triglycerides faster.

22
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:55,520
Cutting refined carbs, drinking less, and moving more — these three habits alone can bring your triglyceride level down noticeably.

23
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:02,400
If your level climbs above 500, the risk of acute pancreatitis rises sharply, so don't ignore a result that high.

24
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:03,680
Talk to your doctor.
